  "answer": "**Final status: passing.** Build, unit and component all pass in the confirming run (`tmp/verification/20261021T142041Z-ccca93`). It took one fix cycle.\n\n**What failed:** The first run passed the build but failed the unit stage (exit code 2), so the component stage never ran. 39 unit tests failed on both net9.0 and net10.0:\n- 34 in `SemanticMatcherTests`: every built-in matcher (`isUuid`, `isIsoDate`, `isEmail`, `greaterThan`, `hasLength`, \u2026), plus the custom-matcher, null-expected, nested-path and missing-field tests.\n- 5 in `ResultMatcherTests`, all of them tests that use a semantic matcher.\n\nEach one threw `ArgumentException: Unknown semantic matcher 'isuuid'`, with the name in lowercase.\n\n**Root cause:** `ParseSpec` in `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs` lowercased the matcher name. The built-in and custom matchers are stored under camelCase names (`isUuid`, `greaterThan`) in a case-sensitive lookup, so no name ever matched. `ValidateSpecs` uses the same parser, so it was broken the same way. The missing-field test also failed, because the name lookup threw before the check that test expects.\n\n**What was changed:** Two lines in `src/ConfIT/Matching/SemanticMatcher.cs`. Matcher names are now matched exactly as written in the DSL:\n- Line 85: `return (spec.ToLowerInvariant(), null);` \u2192 `return (spec, null);`\n- Line 91: `return (spec[..parenIdx].ToLowerInvariant(), \u2026);` \u2192 `return (spec[..parenIdx], \u2026);`\n\nNo tests were edited. They use the documented camelCase names, and none expects names to be case-insensitive.\n\nOne side effect: matcher names are now case-sensitive. A test definition that writes `isuuid` instead of `isUuid` will now fail with \"unknown matcher\". Before the regression, any spelling other than all-lowercase failed, so this only matters if something relied on lowercase names. Nothing in the run did.\n\nI didn't build, run tests, read logs or edit files myself; all of it went through the subagents. Nothing was committed.",
